# Valgrind suppressions for the native-checks valgrind leg. Maintainer-only:
# tools/ is in .Rbuildignore, so this never reaches the tarball, and nothing
# about the shipped build changes because of it.
#
# Read this before adding an entry: a suppression is a promise that a finding
# is understood, not a way to quiet one that is not. Every entry below was
# written against a real stack from a CI artifact, and the comment says which
# behaviour produced it.
#
# Two classes are covered. Everything else still fails the leg.
#
# Why this file exists at all, rather than -DENABLE_MEMCHECK: that is libmdbx's
# switch for finding bugs *inside* libmdbx, not for annotating its page padding
# away. It was tried and reverted -- it raised the uninitialised-value contexts
# from 2 to 13, and it shrinks the default geometry enough to fail the
# reservation test in test-env.R.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. libmdbx writes whole pages, including padding it never initialised.
#
# A page is the unit of I/O, and the bytes past the end of the data in it are
# written as they are. That is libmdbx's design, not a defect, and it predates
# this package -- every frame between the syscall and mdbx_txn_commit_ex is
# vendored. Reported as three contexts: the writev vector has one entry per
# run of contiguous dirty pages, so the index varies with the commit.
#
# Anchored on mdbx_txn_commit_ex so that only the commit path is covered; the
# `...` absorbs the inlined osal_pwrite/osal_ioring_write/iov_write/txn_write
# frames, whose names valgrind resolves inconsistently under -O2.
#
# NOT an attempt to hide a real uninitialised-memory bug in this package: no
# frame of ours appears in any of the stacks, and any finding that reaches a
# pwrite from anywhere other than a commit is still reported.
{
   libmdbx: page padding written by txn_commit (pwritev vector[0])
   Memcheck:Param
   pwritev(vector[0])
   ...
   fun:mdbx_txn_commit_ex
   ...
}
{
   libmdbx: page padding written by txn_commit (pwritev vector[1])
   Memcheck:Param
   pwritev(vector[1])
   ...
   fun:mdbx_txn_commit_ex
   ...
}
{
   libmdbx: page padding written by txn_commit (pwrite)
   Memcheck:Param
   pwrite64(buf)
   ...
   fun:mdbx_txn_commit_ex
   ...
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. An environment or transaction deliberately abandoned after a panic.
#
# When libmdbx raises an assertion failure, this package never hands the handle
# back to it: the close path clears the pointer and returns without calling
# mdbx_env_close(), because re-entering a library that has just rejected its
# own invariants is how a bad situation becomes a crash. See the comment at
# src/r_mdbx.cpp:687. The memory is given up on purpose and the process keeps
# it until it exits.
#
# It is visible as a leak only because the panic tests close a poisoned
# environment, which drops the last pointer to the MDBX_env and moves it from
# "still reachable" to "definitely lost". The behaviour did not change; its
# accounting did.
#
# Honest limit, worth knowing before trusting these: the allocation stack of a
# deliberately abandoned environment is identical to that of one leaked by a
# genuine bug -- the panic happens later and leaves no trace in it. So these
# three entries do cover a real leak of an MDBX_env or MDBX_txn, if one is ever
# introduced on the open or begin path. They are kept narrow (these three
# allocation sites, definite and indirect only) to bound that, and the
# lifecycle tests plus mdbx_env_open_count_() are what actually guard the
# invariant they cannot.
{
   mdbx: MDBX_env abandoned after a libmdbx panic (create)
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:mdbx_env_create
   ...
}
{
   mdbx: MDBX_env internals abandoned after a libmdbx panic (open)
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:mdbx_env_open
   ...
}
{
   mdbx: MDBX_txn abandoned after a libmdbx panic (begin)
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:mdbx_txn_begin_ex
   ...
}
